{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Alias destination census added to the Janitor report (2026-09-01). It maps every inventoried public alias to its canonical config key, CLI command, Python/MCP operation, provider route, or module owner, and expands the internal re-export families by symbol. Key decision fact: bridge-status/bridge-fsck/bridge-probe/jira-onboard/verify-authorship and create_placeholder are thin forwards over canonical owners; the env/config aliases similarly resolve directly into canonical typed fields. The reconcile family is not a one-for-one rename: dry-run maps closest to bridge preview, bootstrap-strict to bridge sync --max-changes 10, bootstrap-throttle to --max-changes 100, live to bridge sync, while reconcile-check has no bridge-preview equivalent and legacy defaults/filter/output contracts remain in a dedicated adapter per ADR 0092. Internal relocation is also not proof of deadness: Mode comparison dunders still call rank(), code-review production still calls run_security_detectors, and plan-review/reconcile facades still carry production callers. Phase-5 deletion must therefore rewire those callers first or limit automatic deletion to aliases whose old facade has no production reachability."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788286858730840001,"uuid":"576421e9-95da-4fa6-b629-f97f5a47f675"}